#{[17592186405578 :question/worked-solution "$5(5)^2+3(12)^2=557$ and $5(3)^2+3(4)^2=93$. Thus $f(B)-f(A)=\\ln\\sqrt{557}-\\ln\\sqrt{93}$. The curve is understood to lie in the field domain, excluding the origin.\n\nSince $557$ and $93$ are positive, the power and quotient rules for logarithms give\n\n$$\\ln\\sqrt{557}-\\ln\\sqrt{93}=\\frac12\\ln557-\\frac12\\ln93=\\frac12\\ln\\left(\\frac{557}{93}\\right).$$"] [17592186537006 :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,dV={∭}_{Δ}\\,f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,z)\\,r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta .\n$$\n\nIf $(r,\\theta ,z)$ are the cylindrical polar coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the formulas\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,z=z.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the projection of $R$ onto the $xy$-plane:\n\n![](images/57/5764f328a614ddaa96abd28a4568c4cd7dfcf0988033d3122bdce0e1e52b7bc5.png)\n\nThe region $R$ is one-half of a right vertical cylinder of radius $4$ whose axis passes through the point $(0,4)$ in the $xy$-plane, and is\n\n- bounded below by $z=-5,$ and\n\n- bounded above by $z=\\sqrt{- x^{2} - y^{2} + 81}=\\sqrt{81 - r^{2}}.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write the ${x}^{2}+(y-4)^{2}=16$ in polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+(y-4)^{2} & =16 \\\\ (r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta)^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta-4)^{2} & =16 \\\\ {r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta -8r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta+16 & =16 \\\\ {r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )-8r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta & =0 \\\\ {r}^{2}-8r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta & =0 \\\\ r(r-8\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire semicircle, we must have $r=8 \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}.$\n\nAs a result, in cylindrical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta ,z)\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 8 \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)},\\,\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\le \\theta \\le \\pi,\\,-5\\le z\\le \\sqrt{81 - r^{2}}\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}2 x y\\,\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{Δ}2(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta)(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta)\\cdot r\\,dz\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\pi}{\\int }_{0}^{8 \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}{\\int }_{-5}^{\\sqrt{81 - r^{2}}}2 r^{3} \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)} \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}\\,dz\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186539359 :answer/value "2 \\ln(4)"] [17592186539364 :answer/value "\\frac{203 \\ln(4)}{2}"] [17592186539370 :answer/value "- 26 \\ln(4)"] [17592186539372 :answer/value "2 \\ln(4)"] [17592186539373 :answer/value "- 14 \\ln(4)"] [17592186539377 :answer/value "14\\operatorname{ln}⁡4"] [17592186539378 :answer/value "26\\operatorname{ln}⁡4"] [17592186539385 :answer/value "42 \\ln(4)"] [17592186539386 :answer/value "\\frac{85 \\ln(4)}{2}"] [17592186539390 :answer/value "78 \\ln(4)"] [17592186548859 :answer/value "-\\frac12\\log_{8}(6)"] [17592186555960 :answer/value "- 2 {e}^{2(t - 2)} \\sin((t - 2)^{2}) (t - 2)"] [17592186555963 :answer/value "4\\left(- 2 {e}^{2(t + 1)} \\sin((t + 1)^{2}) (t + 1)\\right)"] [17592186555970 :answer/value "4\\left(2{e}^{2(t + 1)}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡({(t + 1)}^{2})-(t + 1)\\operatorname{sin}⁡({(t + 1)}^{2}))\\right)"] [17592186555971 :answer/value "2 \\cos((t - 2)^{2}) {e}^{2(t - 2)}"] [17592186555976 :answer/value "4\\left({e}^{2(t + 1)} \\left(2 \\cos((t + 1)^{2}) - \\sin((t + 1)^{2})\\right)\\right)"] [17592186555979 :answer/value "4\\left(2 \\cos((t + 1)^{2}) {e}^{2(t + 1)}\\right)"] [17592186555991 :answer/value "2{e}^{2(t - 2)}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡({(t - 2)}^{2})-(t - 2)\\operatorname{sin}⁡({(t - 2)}^{2}))"] [17592186555992 :answer/value "4\\left({e}^{(t + 1)} \\left(2 \\cos((t + 1)^{2}) - {e}^{(t + 1)} \\sin((t + 